+*   **Output:** A rendered image of the isosurface defined by a threshold value $T$ [S1].
 
 ### Data Structures
 **Min-Max Octree:**
 The octree is built directly around voxels (as opposed to cells) [S5, unofficial]. Each node in the octree stores aggregate information derived from its children:
-*   **Internal Nodes:** Store the minimum ($min$) and maximum ($max$) scalar values of all voxels in their subtree [S3, unofficial][S5, unofficial]. These values are used to determine if the isosurface threshold $T$ lies within the node's range [S3, unofficial]. If the threshold is outside the node's range, the node is empty with respect to the isosurface and can be skipped [S1, S2][S3, unofficial][S6].
-*   **Leaf Nodes:** Store the actual scalar voxel values [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Internal Nodes:** Store the minimum ($min$) and maximum ($max$) scalar values of all voxels in their subtree. These values are used to determine if the isosurface threshold $T$ lies within the node's range. If the threshold $T$ falls outside the stored $[min, max]$ range, the node is pruned [G].
+*   **Leaf Nodes:** Store the actual scalar voxel values [G].
 *   **Construction:** The octree is constructed bottom-up. A common implementation involves creating a MIP texture from a min-max texture used to determine block occupancy [S1][S3, unofficial]. The base level of the octree has equal size along all three cardinal axes [S1][S3, unofficial].
 
 **Voxel Data:**
-The scalar values are stored in a format accessible during traversal [S3, unofficial]. In some implementations, the acceleration tree and scalar data are embedded in a single structure [S4, unofficial]. For GPU implementations, the volume is often stored as a 3D texture [S6].
